{
  "image_name": "0daf6c672e47fb07273933cb8e2081b5.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how fibrinogen, once it enters the brain after blood–brain barrier disruption, is converted into fibrin and drives several harmful CNS responses. At the top, the three fibrinogen chains are mapped with notable binding or cleavage regions, showing that different parts of the molecule interact with receptors and signaling partners. A structural view below shows soluble fibrinogen being cleaved by thrombin into polymerized fibrin.\n\nThe lower schematic links this molecular change to pathology in the neurovascular unit. Leakage of fibrinogen through damaged endothelium appears associated with VE-cadherin/ICAM1 changes and RHOA signaling. In the parenchyma, fibrin engages distinct cell types: astrocytes respond through latent TGFβ/TGFR1 signaling and SMAD2/3, promoting scar formation; neurons show integrin/EGFR-related signaling that inhibits neurite outgrowth; and microglia bind fibrin through CD11b/CD18, activating pathways including RHOA and AKT, leading to inflammation, reactive oxygen species, and axonal damage. On the right, oligodendrocyte precursor cells are shown sensing fibrin via ACVR1 and SMAD1/5/8 signaling, which blocks their differentiation and thereby inhibits remyelination. Overall, the image presents fibrin as a central mediator linking vascular leakage to neuroinflammation, tissue scarring, neuronal injury, and failed repair.",
